From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] dndcp: Use the native Wayland drag for host to guest
 drags

Where waylandDrag/ has connected, DnDUIX11 starts a host to guest drag
as a native Wayland drag and falls back to XDND otherwise.
LocalSetContentProvider() now collects the formats once, and both drag
sources offer the same MIME types and bytes. On the first motion the
plugin arms the overlays, hides the detection window, and presses
through uinput. FakeMouse_Button(), new here, skips the 100 ms pause
that FakeMouse_Click() takes for GTK's drag detection. The host's RPC
messages wait behind any sleep, and the user can let go of a quick drag
in that time.

If a drag cannot start from the press, the button is released and the
detection window is mapped again for the XDND drag, which then waits for
the window manager to adopt the window before it presses. A failure of
WaylandDrag_Init() for a reason other than a missing global, such as a
compositor that has not yet announced its outputs, is retried at the
start of the next host drag.

Send the host drag feedback before starting the drag rather than after.
The host chooses between a drop and a cancel from the feedback it has
when the user lets go, and the user can let go of a quick drag while the
overlay is still being mapped.

The offered URIs must be resolvable while the target decides. They name
vmblock paths, and a lookup under a vmblock entry blocks until the host
sends the file. A file manager that resolves the URI to decide would
stall its own main loop, and one that cannot stat what it is offered
refuses the drop. For every host to guest file drag, native or XDND,
stage an empty placeholder for each file and directory at drag begin,
and add the block at the drop instead, before the button is released.
Thunar on Xfce, on Xorg, refused every XDND drop while the block was in
place from the start. The block then holds the target's read until the
files arrive. The names pass LocalIsSafeStagingName() here as well. When
the placeholders cannot be staged, the drag adds the block at once, as
before. A drag that ends without the host's files removes its
placeholders, which a target would otherwise read as empty files.

The host decides when GET_FILES_DONE arrives, and it can arrive before
the drop. It then cancels the pending block. Otherwise the drop would
add a block that nothing removes, and the application that took the drop
would wait in open() forever. RemoveBlock() now removes the block from
the path it was added on. A block that an earlier drag left on another
directory is released before a new one is added, rather than taken for
the new drag's block.

OnSrcDrop() must not show, raise or warp over the Xwayland detection
window during a native drag. Even after the target has accepted, any of
those makes the compositor cancel the drag instead of dropping. It moves
the pointer and releases the button through uinput only.

The button must be released on the target's current answer. A Gtk target
answers one pointer motion with one accept and two refusals. The host
sends motions about 1.2 ms apart and releases as soon as it stops, so
the last answer is wherever the target had got to, and about a third of
the time that is a refusal. Before releasing, wait for an accept that
stays current for 30 ms, for at most 500 ms per attempt. A target that
refuses still ends the drag when the wait runs out. The block on the
staged files goes in after the wait, since the target stats the files to
decide.

A file manager fetches the offer's contents and decides only on a motion
that comes after they arrive. When the pointer has already stopped, move
it one pixel and back. Thunar can also carry the contents of one drag
into the next, when the pointer crossed its window faster than it kept
up. It then refuses the next drag on files that are gone, without
looking at the new offer, until it sees a leave. A short-lived overlay
on each output takes the drag off Thunar and gives it back. The drag's
own overlay cannot be used for that, because an unmapped layer surface
returns to the unconfigured state, and KWin never configures it again.

Read the Wayland connection between the host's motions too. The motions
come in bursts that the RPC channel handles in one go. When a target
asks for the offer's contents on entering, and the pointer has left it
again before they arrive, GTK reports a failed transfer, and libfm 1.4.1
then dereferences NULL.

A Wayland drag emits none of the GtkDragSource signals, so
OnWaylandDragDone() releases the faked press when the drag ends without
a host drop. Each native drag carries a generation number, so that the
end of an abandoned drag is not taken for the end of the next one. A new
host drag, a reset or a host cancel ends a native drag that is still
running.

In a build without Wayland support waylandDrag/waylandDragStub.c
provides the functions, and a native drag is never available.

Measured with a host driving real drags on sway 1.11, files land in
Thunar 4.20.9 and PCManFM, the target settles in 32 to 43 ms, and a drop
onto no target is discarded. Host drags into Dolphin 26.08.1 on KDE
Plasma 6.7.5 were tested by hand. In nested compositors, start_drag from
a faked press serial and delivery to a native client also work on KWin
6.7.5 and Mutter 50.5, and all three compositors refuse a
wl_pointer.enter serial. The retry of WaylandDrag_Init() and the XDND
fallback after a failed start have not been tested on a VM.
